//? Title: FFT: Butterflies, Spectral Meaning and Convolution
//? Subtitle: Exact small transforms, calibrated amplitudes and finite-record limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A sampled signal can be represented by complex frequency coefficients, but summing
every coefficient directly takes quadratic work. We want the same discrete transform
more cheaply, together with a clear account of signs, normalization and what a finite
record's peaks mean. A four-sample calculation and an eight-sample signal expose those
conventions before leakage, windowing, padding and convolution examples. Direct-DFT,
complex inverse and energy checks test the radix-2 implementation independently. A
256-sample signal then revisits the original two-tone example with its deterministic
interference described as additional sinusoids. All inputs are constructed so that
their frequencies and algebra can be checked; no empirical dataset is needed.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Define the transform before interpreting a spectrum

For N complex samples, the forward and inverse conventions here are

$$
 X_k=\sum_{t=0}^{N-1}x_t e^{-2\pi i kt/N},\qquad
 x_t=\frac1N\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}X_k e^{+2\pi i kt/N}.
$$
\alttext(The forward DFT uses a negative complex exponential without scaling. The inverse uses a positive exponential and divides by the transform length.)

`algorithms::fft` takes a working vector by value and returns all N complex bins.
An lvalue input is unchanged. The inverse flag supplies the positive exponent and
1/N factor. Empty input returns empty and a singleton is identity; other lengths must
be powers of two. `fft_real` also returns all N bins, rather than a packed real-input
half spectrum. This report concerns that radix-2 API, not the separate arbitrary-length
tensor transforms under `nn::fft`.

For sample rate f_s, unshifted bin k represents k*f_s/N up to Nyquist; bins above N/2
represent (k−N)*f_s/N. Real inputs satisfy X[N−k]=conj(X[k]). DC is bin zero; even-N
Nyquist is bin N/2, representing both signs of f_s/2. Frequencies separated by integer
multiples of f_s produce identical sampled complex exponentials. In particular, real
cosines at f and f_s−f alias; an FFT cannot distinguish them from those samples alone.

>> Reuse even and odd transforms

Split t=2r and t=2r+1. Their length-N/2 transforms E and O obey

$$
 X_k=E_k+W_N^k O_k,\qquad X_{k+N/2}=E_k-W_N^k O_k,\qquad
 W_N^k=e^{-2\pi i k/N}.
$$
\alttext(A twiddle-weighted odd-index transform is added to and subtracted from the even-index transform, yielding two output bins.)

The second equality uses W_N^(k+N/2)=−W_N^k. Repeating the split gives log2(N)
levels, each with N/2 butterflies. The iterative implementation first bit-reverses the
input order, then combines length-two blocks, length-four blocks, and so on. Its work
is O(N log N), with N/2*log2(N) butterflies; a direct DFT has N squared summands.
The by-value working vector occupies O(N) storage; after it exists, butterflies update
it in place. These are operation structures, not a claim about a measured speed ratio.

The butterfly diagram is built from retained TikZ source. Regeneration needs LaTeX
with PGF/TikZ, standalone and Latin Modern, plus dvisvgm. Exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/fft_butterfly.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/fft_butterfly.tikz > build/fft_butterfly.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/fft_butterfly.svg build/fft_butterfly.dvi > build/fft_butterfly.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local FFT butterfly diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local FFT butterfly diagram.
// \image(figures/fft_butterfly.svg)
// result_end
\caption(One twiddle-weighted odd contribution supplies a sum and a difference.)
\alttext(Even and odd sub-transform values feed two outputs. The odd branch multiplies by the forward twiddle, then joins the even branch with plus and minus signs to produce bins k and k plus N over two.)

>> Preserve energy and distinguish magnitude from amplitude

Orthogonality of the complex exponential basis gives Parseval's identity:

$$
 \sum_t|x_t|^2=\frac1N\sum_k|X_k|^2.
$$
\alttext(Time-domain squared magnitude sums equal the frequency-domain squared magnitude sum divided by N.)

A round trip alone can miss paired convention errors, so `fft_diagnostics` compares
forward coefficients with a separate direct sum, checks the full complex inverse
residual, and checks energy. Its direct reference uses O(N squared) work and extended
accumulation. It is deliberately a small-input correctness check, not a fast transform.
The factorization is exact algebra; finite arithmetic still has roundoff. The low-level
FFT propagates nonfinite arithmetic, while the diagnostics reject nonfinite inputs and
unrepresentable results.

For M real observed samples and window weights w, zero-pad to a power-of-two N>=M.
Write W=sum(w), U=sum(w squared), and a_k=1 at DC and Nyquist, two elsewhere. The
one-sided summary uses

$$
 A_k=\frac{a_k|X_k|}{W},\qquad Q_k=\frac{a_k|X_k|^2}{NU},\qquad
 \sum_{k=0}^{N/2}Q_k=\frac{\sum_{t=0}^{M-1}w_t^2x_t^2}{U}.
$$
\alttext(Amplitude divides by the sum of observed window weights and doubles only interior bins. Per-bin mean-square contributions use the squared-window sum and integrate to window-weighted mean square.)

For an isolated coherent interior cosine, A recovers its amplitude. It is not an exact
amplitude estimator for an off-bin mixture. DC and Nyquist are not doubled. Q is a
mean-square contribution per bin, not power spectral density per hertz. For a rectangular
window, W=U=M; neither denominator counts appended zeros. For periodic Hann,
w_t=.5−.5*cos(2*pi*t/M), with M in the denominator rather than the symmetric M−1
window convention. The window trades lower distant leakage for a wider main lobe.

>> A finite record defines a frequency grid, not unlimited resolution

A record spanning T=M/f_s has a frequency scale 1/T. A tone that is not periodic on
that record spreads across DFT bins under rectangular truncation. This leakage is a
property of the finite observation/window, not an FFT implementation error. Padding
changes grid spacing from f_s/M to f_s/N and samples the same finite-record transform
more densely. It adds no observed duration, no new samples and no intrinsic ability
to resolve arbitrarily close tones. Windows and coherent-gain correction do not
remove aliasing or make off-bin peak estimates universally exact.

>> Multiplication in frequency wraps unless padding prevents it

Inverse-transforming the product of two length-N DFTs gives circular convolution:

$$
 y_t^{\mathrm{circ}}=\sum_{s=0}^{N-1}a_s b_{(t-s)\bmod N}.
$$
\alttext(Circular convolution combines samples with their indices taken modulo the transform period.)

For input lengths L and H, full linear convolution has L+H−1 samples. Padding to at
least that length makes the circular result equal the linear one on its support.
`fft_linear_convolution` chooses the smallest sufficient power of two, multiplies
coefficients, applies the normalized inverse and trims the result. It returns real
parts, discarding imaginary roundoff. Its O(N log N) work and O(N) storage include
padding; an empty operand gives an empty result.

> Simple examples

>> Four samples expose the complex signs

For x=(1,2,3,4), even samples (1,3) give E=(4,−2) and odd samples
(2,4) give O=(6,−2). At k=1 the twiddle is −i, so E_1+(−i)O_1=−2+2i.
The full result is (10,−2+2i,−2,−2−2i). Spectral squared magnitudes sum to 120;
dividing by four returns time energy 30. The table preserves imaginary signs rather
than showing only magnitudes, and the diagnostic includes imaginary inverse residuals.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fft_four_samples.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::algorithms;
  const std::vector<double> x           = {1, 2, 3, 4};
  const auto                transformed = fft_real(x);
  std::vector<double>       real, imaginary;
  for (auto z : transformed) {
    real.push_back(z.real());
    imaginary.push_back(z.imag());
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("bin", std::vector<double>{0, 1, 2, 3});
  table.add_column("real", real);
  table.add_column("imaginary", imaginary);
  auto signal = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), coefficients = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  signal.bars({0, 1, 2, 3}, x, "samples", {37, 99, 235}, .5)
      .title("Four observed samples")
      .x_label("sample index")
      .y_label("value")
      .y_limits(0, 6);
  coefficients.line({0, 1, 2, 3}, real, "real part", {37, 99, 235})
      .points({0, 1, 2, 3}, real, "real bins", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .line({0, 1, 2, 3}, imaginary, "imaginary part", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Forward sign is visible at bin one")
      .x_label("unshifted bin index")
      .y_label("coefficient value")
      .y_limits(-4, 15);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(signal)
      .add(coefficients)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/fft_four_samples.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;
  fmt.digits     = 6;
  fmt.scientific = {
      "max_dft_error",
      "relative_dft_error",
      "max_inverse_error",
      "relative_inverse_error",
      "relative_energy_error"};
  std::print(
      "{}\n\n{}\n",
      table.to_md(6),
      fft_diagnostics(std::vector<std::complex<double>>(x.begin(), x.end())).to_md(fmt));
}
```
