\caption(Endpoint weighting and sample-count normalization turn raw FFT magnitudes into coherent amplitudes.)
\alttext(Eight time samples combine an offset, a one-hertz cosine and Nyquist alternation. Spectrum bars recover amplitudes two, three and one half. The table includes the Nyquist bin and mean-square contributions summing to 8.75.)

>> The same observation can have a denser frequency grid

Observe a 5.5 Hz unit cosine for 64 samples at 64 samples per second: only one
second of data. The 64-point rectangular DFT cannot place a bin at 5.5 Hz; periodic
Hann lowers distant side lobes while broadening the main lobe. Padding the same
rectangular record to 512 adds bins spaced .125 Hz, including 5.5, but the observed
duration is unchanged. Its coherent denominator remains 64. The left panel displays
20*log10(amplitude) floored at −80 dB solely for plotting; the right uses raw amplitudes.
The table retains original-grid and padded-grid peak values without calling the finer
grid a longer observation or a new resolution guarantee.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/fft_leakage.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  using namespace dm::algorithms;
  std::vector<double> x(64);
  for (int t = 0; t < 64; ++t)
    x[t] = std::cos(2 * std::numbers::pi_v<double> * 5.5 * t / 64);
  const auto rect = fft_spectrum(x, 64), hann = fft_spectrum(x, 64, 0, FftWindow::PeriodicHann),
             padded = fft_spectrum(x, 64, 512);
  auto db           = [](const auto& s) {
    auto v = s.numeric_column("amplitude").values();
    for (auto& a : v)
      a = std::max(-80., 20 * std::log10(std::max(1e-300, a)));
    return v;
  };
  auto leakage = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), grid = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  leakage.line(rect.numeric_column("frequency").values(), db(rect), "rectangular", {37, 99, 235})
      .line(hann.numeric_column("frequency").values(), db(hann), "periodic Hann", {220, 38, 38})
      .title("Window choice changes the leakage pattern")
      .x_label("frequency (Hz)")
      .y_label("amplitude dB, display floor -80")
      .y_limits(-80, 10);
  const auto zoom   = padded.filter([](const auto& r) {
    return r.get_double("frequency") >= 2 && r.get_double("frequency") <= 9;
  }),
             coarse = rect.filter([](const auto& r) {
               return r.get_double("frequency") >= 2 && r.get_double("frequency") <= 9;
             });
  grid.line(
          zoom.numeric_column("frequency").values(),
          zoom.numeric_column("amplitude").values(),
          "512-point grid",
          {37, 99, 235})
      .points(
          coarse.numeric_column("frequency").values(),
          coarse.numeric_column("amplitude").values(),
          "64-point bins",
          {220, 38, 38},
          5)
      .title("Padding interpolates the finite record")
      .x_label("frequency (Hz), still 64 observations")
      .y_label("one-sided amplitude")
      .y_limits(0, 1.4);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(leakage).add(grid).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/fft_leakage.svg");
  std::print(
      "64 observed samples in each case.\n\nOriginal-grid bins near the tone:\n\n{}\n\nPadded-grid "
      "bin at 5.5 Hz:\n\n{}\n",
      rect.slice(4, 8).to_md(9),
      padded.slice(44, 45).to_md(9));
}
```
