\caption(An FFT identifies the finite mixture’s coefficients; an inverse recovers that mixture rather than removing its extra tones.)
\alttext(The 256-sample waveform sits beside calibrated amplitudes through 64 hertz. A table reports the five largest non-DC bins without altering coefficients, followed by numerical checks and the analytically derived interference frequencies.)

> Conclusion

The small calculations establish the forward sign, inverse scale, conjugate symmetry,
DC/Nyquist weighting and Parseval normalization. Leakage and padding concern a finite
record's interpretation; linear and circular convolution require different padding
choices. The longer signal contains known coherent tones and deterministic off-bin
interference. Its inverse check tests reconstruction of the entire mixture, while
independent direct sums check the coefficient convention. These results establish
numerical agreement on the reported signals, not exact floating-point arithmetic,
noise removal, unlimited resolution or a timing claim on arbitrary hardware.

> References

- Cooley, J. W. and Tukey, J. W. (1965). [An Algorithm for the Machine Calculation of Complex Fourier Series|https://www.cs.jhu.edu/~misha/ReadingSeminar/Papers/Cooley65.pdf]. Mathematics of Computation 19(90), 297–301. doi:10.1090/S0025-5718-1965-0178586-1.
- FFTW authors. [The one-dimensional DFT|https://www.fftw.org/fftw3_doc/The-1d-Discrete-Fourier-Transform-_0028DFT_0029.html]. Exponent convention and unnormalized transforms; this library additionally normalizes its inverse by N.
- NumPy developers. [rfft|https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.fft.rfft.html]. Hermitian redundancy and the special DC/Nyquist endpoints; the native fft_real here returns the full spectrum.
- SciPy developers. [Spectral analysis|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/tutorial/signal.html] and [Hann window|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.windows.hann.html]. Window normalization, spectral leakage and zero-padding conventions.
- SciPy developers. [fftconvolve|https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.fftconvolve.html]. Full linear convolution and FFT-based implementation.
